  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 409 PM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nVisible satellite shows clearing skies across much of Upper \nMichigan this afternoon as drier air works into the region behind a \ndeparting clipper low now located near the southern tip of James Bay \nper latest RAP analysis. Breezy northwest winds continue in a cold \nair advection regime in the wake of the low, with gales over the \neastern half of Lake Superior and winds gusting to 25-35 mph over \nadjacent land areas and in the Keweenaw. A reinforcing shot of cold \nair will arrive over Lake Superior tonight as the upper trough \ndeepens over eastern Canada, sending 850 mb temps below 0 C. As a \nresult, could see some light lake effect rain showers redevelop over \nthe eastern extent of the UP tonight into early Monday as lake \nsurface temperatures continue to reside around 13-15 C in the east. \nWith surface temps falling into the low to mid 30s in a few spots \novernight, can't rule out seeing the first snowflakes of the season \nmixing in for inland areas in the east, with no accumulation. In \nterms of rainfall, do not expect much if any measurable \nprecipitation from these showers through Monday. Otherwise, Monday \nis shaping up to be a pleasant fall day with plenty of sunshine over \nmost of the area and temps holding in the 50s to perhaps low 60s \nnear the WI border.\n\nAnother weak disturbance will approach the area Monday night into \nTuesday ahead of the next trough dropping out of central Canada. A \nrelative lack of forcing and plenty of mid level dry air to overcome \nper model soundings will serve to limit rain chances to 20-30% for \nnow. Once again can't rule out a few snowflakes mixing in with rain \nMonday night, this time in the interior west, but meager QPF values \nof a few hundredths of an inch will serve to limit any meaningful \nprecipitation. Temperatures then rebound into the 60s across the \narea on Tuesday amidst low level southerly flow. Cold advection then \nquickly returns Tuesday night into Wednesday in the wake of the \nsystem, kicking off another round of potential lake effect rain \nshowers and breezy conditions during the middle of the week. Surface \nridging then favors drier weather later in the week before another \nlow then potentially brings additional rainfall chances to the area \nnext weekend.",
